{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_smnr6b7r4fmocc5fptxsxpyfqtv3igk6jvmzv3k2owa4ax3knkja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01FA85rBLNNeuAVDnYmcCEtx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-uE7TUY/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md","content":"# SYMBOLS.md — Phase A: public symbol surface\n\n## How this was produced\n\nThe C project (`c_src/CMakeLists.txt`) contains exactly one translation unit,\n`c_src/src/main.c`, and builds it as an executable:\n\n```cmake\ncmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.10)\nproject(driver)\nadd_executable(driver src/main.c)\n```\n\nTo get a comparable shared object (without modifying anything in `c_src/`) the\nsame single translation unit is compiled with `-shared -fPIC`:\n\n```sh\ncc -shared -fPIC -o libdriver_c.so c_src/src/main.c\n```\n\nThe Rust crate was given a `[lib]` target with `crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"]`\n(`src/lib.rs`) whose only purpose is to re-export the identical C ABI surface;\n`src/imp.rs` holds the translation and is shared verbatim between the cdylib and\nthe `driver` binary, so the two can never drift.\n\n## `nm -D --defined-only` on the C `.so`\n\n```\n0000000000001160 T driver\n0000000000001070 T main\n```\n\nUndefined / imported symbols in the C `.so` (libc, not part of the surface):\n`printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`, `__isoc99_scanf@GLIBC_2.7`, plus the weak toolchain\nsymbols `_ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable`, `_ITM_registerTMCloneTable`,\n`__cxa_finalize@GLIBC_2.2.5`, `__gmon_start__`.\n\n## `nm -D --defined-only` on the Rust `.so`\n\n```\n0000000000015650 T driver\n0000000000015670 T main\n```\n\n## Parity table\n\n| # | C symbol | C type | signature (from `c_src/src/main.c`) | exported by Rust `.so` | Rust definition | status |\n|---|----------|--------|------------------------------------|------------------------|-----------------|--------|\n| 1 | `driver` | `T` (global text) | `void driver(int x)` | yes, `T` | `#[no_mangle] pub extern \"C\" fn driver(x: c_int)` in `src/lib.rs` | ✅ match |\n| 2 | `main`   | `T` (global text) | `int main(void)` (K&R empty parameter list) | yes, `T` | `#[no_mangle] pub extern \"C\" fn main() -> c_int` in `src/lib.rs` | ✅ match |\n\nThere are no macro-generated symbols, no global/static data objects, no weak\naliases and no versioned symbols in the C source, so the table above is the\ncomplete surface.\n\n## Missing-symbol analysis\n\n* Symbols exported by the C `.so` but **missing from the Rust `.so`: none.**\n* No C source file was left untranslated: `c_src/src/main.c` is the only C file\n  in the project (`find c_src -name '*.c' -o -name '*.h'` yields just that one\n  file) and both of its functions (`driver`, `main`) are translated in\n  `src/imp.rs` and exported from `src/lib.rs`.\n* No stubs, `todo!()` or `unimplemented!()` were introduced.\n\nThe Rust `.so` additionally exports the usual Rust/`std` runtime symbols\n(`rust_eh_personality`, `__rust_*` allocator shims, etc.). Extra symbols are\nharmless — the requirement is that every C symbol is present in Rust with the\nexact same name, which holds.\n\n## Verification command\n\n`./check_symbols.sh` recomputes both symbol sets and diffs them; it exits\nnon-zero if any C symbol is absent from the Rust `.so`.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":952,"cache_read_input_tokens":46384,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":952,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"759a51e0-0b19-4394-ad6e-11c06fea80b5","uuid":"5f74b104-daf9-4dea-b6e5-f1e446da83f2","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:30:27.347Z"}
