{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01WD2VEVrTU6trNAwUHgrGhE","type":"tool_result","content":"[package]\nname = \"driver\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n# The C project (c_src/CMakeLists.txt) builds `src/main.c` as an executable.\n# We keep an equivalent Rust executable, and additionally expose the exact same\n# public C symbols (`driver`, `main`) from a cdylib so that the translation can\n# be differentially tested through the FFI boundary with `libloading`, exactly\n# the way an external C caller would use it.\n[lib]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n# The library exports a `#[no_mangle] main`, exactly like the C shared object\n# does, so it cannot host libtest's generated entry point. All testing happens\n# through the integration tests in `tests/`, which load the shared objects.\ntest = false\ndoctest = false\n\n[[bin]]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/main.rs\"\n\n[features]\ndefault = []\n\n# Both differential suites redirect file descriptor 1 to capture what the two\n# shared objects write; that is process-global state, so the cases must run\n# sequentially and nothing else may write to fd 1 meanwhile.  libtest's\n# multi-threaded progress output would corrupt the captures, hence our own\n# sequential harness (see `common::run_cases`).\n[[test]]\nname = \"phase_b_configs\"\npath = \"tests/phase_b_configs.rs\"\nharness = false\n\n[[test]]\nname = \"phase_c_errors\"\npath = \"tests/phase_c_errors.rs\"\nharness = false\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]\n=== lib.rs ===\n// C-ABI surface of the translation of c_src/src/main.c.\n//\n// `nm -D` on the shared library built from the C source exports exactly two\n// symbols: `driver` and `main`.  Both are re-exported here with the same names\n// and the same C signatures so that an external caller (and the differential\n// test suite) cannot tell the two libraries apart.\n\n#[path = \"imp.rs\"]\npub mod imp;\n\nuse std::os::raw::c_int;\n\n/// `void driver(int x)`\n#[no_mangle]\npub extern \"C\" fn driver(x: c_int) {\n    imp::driver_stdout(x as i32);\n}\n\n/// `int main(void)`\n#[no_mangle]\npub extern \"C\" fn main() -> c_int {\n    imp::c_main() as c_int\n}\n=== final tree ===\n./CONFIGS.md\n./Cargo.lock\n./Cargo.toml\n./ERRORS.md\n./SYMBOLS.md\n./VERIFICATION.md\n./c_src/CMakeLists.txt\n./c_src/src/main.c\n./check_all_features.sh\n./check_symbols.sh\n./logs/prompt.md\n./logs/translation.log\n./run_difftests.sh\n./src/imp.rs\n./src/lib.rs\n./src/main.rs\n./tests/common/mod.rs\n./tests/phase_b_configs.rs\n./tests/phase_c_errors.rs\n./translation.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"759a51e0-0b19-4394-ad6e-11c06fea80b5","uuid":"22c34340-a5b8-4fb0-bec5-7c46f409cf49","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:55:58.348Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[package]\nname = \"driver\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n# The C project (c_src/CMakeLists.txt) builds `src/main.c` as an executable.\n# We keep an equivalent Rust executable, and additionally expose the exact same\n# public C symbols (`driver`, `main`) from a cdylib so that the translation can\n# be differentially tested through the FFI boundary with `libloading`, exactly\n# the way an external C caller would use it.\n[lib]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n# The library exports a `#[no_mangle] main`, exactly like the C shared object\n# does, so it cannot host libtest's generated entry point. All testing happens\n# through the integration tests in `tests/`, which load the shared objects.\ntest = false\ndoctest = false\n\n[[bin]]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/main.rs\"\n\n[features]\ndefault = []\n\n# Both differential suites redirect file descriptor 1 to capture what the two\n# shared objects write; that is process-global state, so the cases must run\n# sequentially and nothing else may write to fd 1 meanwhile.  libtest's\n# multi-threaded progress output would corrupt the captures, hence our own\n# sequential harness (see `common::run_cases`).\n[[test]]\nname = \"phase_b_configs\"\npath = \"tests/phase_b_configs.rs\"\nharness = false\n\n[[test]]\nname = \"phase_c_errors\"\npath = \"tests/phase_c_errors.rs\"\nharness = false\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]\n=== lib.rs ===\n// C-ABI surface of the translation of c_src/src/main.c.\n//\n// `nm -D` on the shared library built from the C source exports exactly two\n// symbols: `driver` and `main`.  Both are re-exported here with the same names\n// and the same C signatures so that an external caller (and the differential\n// test suite) cannot tell the two libraries apart.\n\n#[path = \"imp.rs\"]\npub mod imp;\n\nuse std::os::raw::c_int;\n\n/// `void driver(int x)`\n#[no_mangle]\npub extern \"C\" fn driver(x: c_int) {\n    imp::driver_stdout(x as i32);\n}\n\n/// `int main(void)`\n#[no_mangle]\npub extern \"C\" fn main() -> c_int {\n    imp::c_main() as c_int\n}\n=== final tree ===\n./CONFIGS.md\n./Cargo.lock\n./Cargo.toml\n./ERRORS.md\n./SYMBOLS.md\n./VERIFICATION.md\n./c_src/CMakeLists.txt\n./c_src/src/main.c\n./check_all_features.sh\n./check_symbols.sh\n./logs/prompt.md\n./logs/translation.log\n./run_difftests.sh\n./src/imp.rs\n./src/lib.rs\n./src/main.rs\n./tests/common/mod.rs\n./tests/phase_b_configs.rs\n./tests/phase_c_errors.rs\n./translation.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_pohudhst3oxqy7gb64ndigg3awy32ntdxjppqj4hqusuxubqnrra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` contains a single 36-line translation unit exporting exactly two symbols: `void driver(int)` (prints `i j\\n` for `i` in `0..x`, `j = 2i`) and `int main(void)` (`scanf(\"%d\")` → `driver(x)`).\n\n## Phase A — surface mapping\n\n| artifact | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | `nm -D` on both `.so`s. C exports `driver`, `main`; Rust now exports both. Missing set is **empty**. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 25 rows, derived by grepping the C for every rejection construct (the source has exactly **one** guard, `i < x`, plus every `scanf`/`printf` failure mode). Documents why null-pointer and out-of-range-enum rows can't exist here, and what maps onto them instead. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 13 axes → 32 rows. `[features]` has no optional features and CMake has no options, so there is exactly **one** feature combination. |\n\nTo make FFI-level testing possible I added a `cdylib` `[lib]` with `#[no_mangle]` `driver`/`main`, sharing `src/imp.rs` verbatim with the binary so they can't drift.\n\n## Phases B/C/D — results\n\n- **Phase B: 32/32** rows green (~1 000 randomized inputs, fixed seeds). `driver` is called directly via `dlopen`/`dlsym` with fd 1 redirected; `main` via `dlsym` in a fresh child per input (matching C's one-`scanf`-per-process reality); plus end-to-end executable comparisons.\n- **Phase C: 29/29** green, each comparing stdout bytes **and** exact wait status (code + signal).\n- **Phase D:** symbol diff empty in both `debug` and `release`; no undefined non-libc symbols. `run_difftests.sh` exits `ALL CHECKS PASSED`.\n\n## Two real bugs found\n\n1. **SIGPIPE divergence (fixed).** Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN`, so `./driver | head -1` exited 0 while the C program dies with signal 13 (status 141). `src/main.rs` now restores `SIG_DFL` — only in the binary, since the `.so`'s `main` must inherit the host's disposition like the C `.so` does.\n\n2. **The test suite was initially lying.** `cargo test` builds the rlib and binaries but **not** the `cdylib`, so every `.so` comparison ran against a stale `libdriver.so`. Mutation testing exposed it: breaking `imp.rs` still \"passed\". The harness now builds the cdylib itself and hard-fails if any artifact is older than `src/`.\n\nI mutation-tested the suite afterwards (table in `ERRORS.md`): 7 mutations are caught; the 3 survivors are provably equivalent mutants (e.g. overflow saturating to `LONG_MAX` vs `0` — both truncate to non-positive `int`, and *all* `x <= 0` produce identical empty output)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1534,"cache_read_input_tokens":191961,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1534,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"759a51e0-0b19-4394-ad6e-11c06fea80b5","uuid":"b619ad8a-2d29-4e11-867a-83c2e243f9eb","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:56:14.267Z"}
